  "enhanced_information": "The clinical presentation of bilious vomiting, abdominal distension, and a contrast enema showing a transition point at the transverse colon suggests a mechanical bowel obstruction. This is supported by the evidence path linking bilious vomiting as a manifestation of obstruction. The location of the transition point in the transverse colon, rather than the rectosigmoid area, makes Hirschsprung disease (associated with failure of neural crest cell migration) less likely. Additionally, while CFTR gene mutations cause cystic fibrosis, which can lead to meconium ileus in neonates, the specific symptoms and enema findings in this case do not align directly with meconium ileus, typically presenting earlier and not specifically at the transverse colon. Other options such as muscle hypertrophy and meiotic nondisjunction are not supported by the clinical evidence or typical presentations of related conditions in neonates. Therefore, the most likely etiology in this scenario is mechanical bowel obstruction."
